# General Instructions

Be brief.

When reporting information to me, be extremely concise and sacrifice grammar for the sake of concision.
Don't be overly eager to agree with me, but think about the suggestions I make and speak up if you have another idea (better or worse).

## Language and Style
— Always use British English
— Always answer in English, even if I ask in another language, unless I specifically request a response in that language.

## Commits and Branches
Format: `<jira-ticket>: <type>(scope): <description>`
Branch: `feature/<jira-ticket>-...`

Rules:
— Imperative present tense ("add" not "added")
— No capitalised first letter, no trailing period
— Body: max 10 bullet points, focus on "why" not "what"
— Only create feature branches when asked, or when on main/master/release/develop
— When on `gitbutler/workspace` branch, use GitButler tooling for all git operations (commit, branch, push) — never use raw git checkout/branch commands

## Keep It Minimal
— No features, abstractions, or error handling beyond what was asked
— Don't touch adjacent code, comments, or formatting
— Match existing style; every changed line traces to the request
— Clean up only what YOUR changes made unused

## Tool Preferences
— Prefer `fd` over `find` for file discovery
— Prefer `rg` (ripgrep) over `grep` for content search
— Prefer `bat` over `cat` for file preview (syntax highlighting, line numbers)
— Use `jq` for JSON processing, never manual string parsing
— Use `duckdb` for ad-hoc SQL queries on CSV, Parquet, or JSON files — no Python scripts or jq chains
— Use `mise run` for task running when a `mise.toml` exists; fall back to `just` (Justfile) over Makefile otherwise
— Use `./gradlew` (or `./mvnw`), never system-installed build tools — but prefer `mise run`/`just` over direct Gradle/Maven commands when task definitions exist
— Use `./gradlew dependencies` for dependency trees, not manual POM/build file reading
— Use `semgrep --config auto` for security/correctness checks on Java/Kotlin before manual review
— Use `shellcheck` to lint shell scripts
— Use `git-delta` is configured as the git pager — diffs have line numbers and structured output
— Use `watchexec` to auto-rerun commands on file changes (e.g. `watchexec -e kt,java -- mise run test`)
— Use `httpie` (`http`/`https` commands) over `curl` for API testing

## Java/Kotlin
— Prefer Kotlin for new files unless the project is Java-only
— Prefer Kotlin DSL (`*.gradle.kts`) for new build scripts
— Run existing formatter (ktfmt/ktlint/spotless) before committing; match project config
